                                {
                                    type: "concept",
                                    title: "What Are Heuristics?",
                                    content: "Heuristics are mental shortcuts that help us make decisions quickly. They evolved because our ancestors needed fast decisions (Is that a predator?) not perfect ones.\n\nKahneman identified three main heuristics:\n\n• **Representativeness**: Judging probability by similarity\n• **Availability**: Judging frequency by how easily examples come to mind\n• **Anchoring**: Using the first number you hear as a reference point\n\nThese shortcuts work well most of the time, but fail in predictable ways."
                                },
                                {
                                    type: "concept",
                                    title: "The Representativeness Heuristic",
                                    content: "We judge probability by how much something 'looks like' our mental prototype.\n\n**The Linda Problem**: Linda is 31, single, outspoken, very bright. As a student, she was deeply concerned with discrimination and social justice.\n\nWhich is more probable?\nA) Linda is a bank teller\nB) Linda is a bank teller AND active in the feminist movement\n\n85% of people choose B - but this is mathematically impossible! The probability of two things both being true can't exceed the probability of just one being true. We're fooled because B 'feels' more like Linda."
                                },
                                {
                                    type: "visual",
                                    title: "Base Rate Neglect",
                                    content: "Imagine 1,000 people:\n• 995 are librarians\n• 5 are farmers\n\nSteve is meek, tidy, has a need for order, and a passion for detail.\n\nIs Steve more likely a librarian or farmer?\n\nMost people say librarian (fits the stereotype). But there are 199 librarians for every farmer! Even if only 10% of librarians fit this description, that's still 99 librarians vs maybe 4 farmers who fit.\n\n**We ignore base rates when stereotypes feel compelling.**"
                                },
                                {
                                    type: "example",
                                    title: "Real-World Impact",
                                    content: "**Medical Diagnosis**: Doctors often overweight symptoms that 'look like' a disease while ignoring how rare that disease actually is.\n\n**Hiring**: Interviewers choose candidates who 'seem like' successful employees, ignoring the actual success rate of similar hires.\n\n**Investing**: We think companies with impressive founders will be successful because they 'look like' winners, ignoring how many similar companies fail.\n\n**Jury Decisions**: Defendants who 'look like' criminals are more likely to be convicted, regardless of evidence."
                                },
                                {
                                    type: "application",
                                    title: "How to Fight Your Biases",
                                    content: "1. **Always ask**: What's the base rate? How common is this outcome in general?\n\n2. **Separate prediction from description**: Just because someone 'looks like' something doesn't mean they are.\n\n3. **Think statistically**: If 1 in 1000 people have a rare disease, and a test is 99% accurate, a positive result still means you probably DON'T have it.\n\n4. **Delay judgment**: Give System 2 time to override System 1's quick stereotypes."
                                },
                                {
                                    type: "quiz",
                                    question: "In the Linda problem, why do most people incorrectly choose 'bank teller AND feminist'?",
                                    options: [
                                        { text: "Because they can't do math", correct: false },
                                        { text: "Because the combination 'represents' Linda better than just bank teller", correct: true },
                                        { text: "Because it's actually the correct answer", correct: false },
                                        { text: "Because they weren't paying attention", correct: false }
                                    ],
                                    explanation: "This is the representativeness heuristic in action. The combined description feels more 'representative' of what we imagine Linda to be, even though it's mathematically less probable. We substitute the question 'what is probable?' with 'what is similar?'"
                                }
