                                    type: "example",
                                    title: "The Meeting After the Meeting",
                                    content: "A company noticed a pattern: polite executive meetings followed by 'meetings after the meeting' where real opinions emerged in hallways and parking lots. Decisions made in the boardroom were quietly undermined because no one had voiced their real concerns. By explicitly inviting conflict in meetings—even assigning a 'devil's advocate'—they brought underground debates into the open where issues could be resolved."
