                                    type: "concept",
                                    title: "The First Three Minutes",
                                    content: "The earliest moments of the universe were unimaginably violent and transformative:\n\n**10â»â´³ seconds (Planck Era)**: Physics as we know it breaks down. Quantum gravity dominates.\n\n**10â»³â¶ seconds (Inflation)**: The universe expands exponentially—doubling in size at least 80 times in a fraction of a second. A region smaller than a proton becomes larger than the observable universe today.\n\n**10â»â¶ seconds**: Quarks combine to form protons and neutrons.\n\n**3 minutes**: **Nucleosynthesis** occurs. Protons and neutrons fuse to create the first atomic nuclei—mostly hydrogen and helium. This process stops after about 20 minutes as the universe cools.\n\n**380,000 years**: The universe cools enough for electrons to join nuclei, forming the first atoms. Light can finally travel freely—this is the CMB we detect today.\n\nEvery hydrogen atom in water is a relic from those first minutes."
