                                    type: "example",
                                    title: "Seeing the Big Bang",
                                    content: "In 1964, Arno Penzias and Robert Wilson at Bell Labs were trying to eliminate static from their radio antenna. No matter what they did—even removing pigeon droppings from the dish—a faint hiss persisted from every direction in the sky.\n\nThey had accidentally discovered the **Cosmic Microwave Background (CMB)**—the oldest light in the universe, released when the cosmos was just 380,000 years old. This 'noise' is the thermal radiation from the hot early universe, now cooled to just 2.7 Kelvin.\n\nThe CMB is remarkably uniform, but contains tiny temperature fluctuations (about 1 part in 100,000). These fluctuations are the seeds of all cosmic structure—the slightly denser regions that would eventually become galaxies, stars, and planets.\n\nPenzias and Wilson won the 1978 Nobel Prize for their accidental discovery of the universe's baby picture."
