                                    type: "concept",
                                    title: "Inflation: The Ultimate Expansion",
                                    content: "One of the Big Bang's most mind-bending aspects is **cosmic inflation**—a period of exponential expansion in the first fraction of a second.\n\n**The Problem Inflation Solves:**\n• Why is the CMB so uniform? Regions on opposite sides of the sky have the same temperature, but light hasn't had time to travel between them\n• Inflation stretched a tiny, causally connected region to cosmic scales before expansion slowed\n\n**How Fast?**\n• During inflation, space doubled in size every 10â»³â· seconds\n• In about 10â»³² seconds, the universe grew from smaller than a proton to larger than the observable universe today\n• This expansion was faster than light—which is allowed because space itself was expanding, not objects moving through space\n\n**What Drove It?**\n• A hypothetical **inflaton field** in an unstable high-energy state\n• When this field decayed, its energy converted to matter and radiation—the hot Big Bang we observe"
