                                    type: "concept",
                                    title: "Extreme Effects Near Black Holes",
                                    content: "Black holes create the most extreme gravitational environments in the universe:\n\n**Time Dilation**\n• Time slows down in strong gravitational fields (general relativity)\n• Near a black hole, time nearly stops relative to distant observers\n• Falling into a black hole: you experience normal time, but the universe appears to speed up enormously outside\n\n**Spaghettification**\n• Tidal forces (gravity gradient) stretch objects vertically and compress horizontally\n• Near stellar black holes, you'd be stretched into a long, thin strand\n• Supermassive black holes have gentler tidal forces at the horizon—you could cross without being torn apart\n\n**Gravitational Lensing**\n• Mass bends spacetime, curving light paths\n• Black holes create dramatic lensing effects\n• You could see the back of your own head if positioned right!\n\n**Frame Dragging**\n• Rotating black holes drag spacetime around with them\n• Near the **ergosphere**, space itself rotates; standing still is impossible\n• Energy can be extracted from rotating black holes (Penrose process)"
