                                    type: "concept",
                                    title: "Hawking Radiation: Black Holes Aren't Forever",
                                    content: "In 1974, Stephen Hawking made a stunning prediction: **black holes slowly evaporate**.\n\n**How It Works (Simplified):**\n• Quantum mechanics allows particle-antiparticle pairs to pop into existence briefly\n• Near the event horizon, one particle can fall in while the other escapes\n• The escaping particle carries away energy\n• From the black hole's perspective, it loses mass\n\n**Properties:**\n• Temperature is inversely proportional to mass: smaller = hotter\n• Stellar black holes: ~10â»â¸ K (colder than the CMB—they're currently growing, not shrinking)\n• As black holes shrink, they heat up and radiate faster\n• Final moments: explosive burst of particles\n\n**Timescales:**\n• Stellar black hole (10 M•‰): ~10â¶â· years to evaporate\n• Supermassive black hole: ~10Â¹â°â° years\n• (The universe is only 10Â¹â° years old)\n\n**The Information Paradox:**\n• If black holes evaporate, what happens to information about what fell in?\n• This remains one of physics' greatest unsolved problems"
