                                    type: "intro",
                                    title: "Vast Collections of Stars",
                                    content: "On a dark night, you might see a faint, hazy band stretching across the sky—the Milky Way, our home galaxy. For millennia, people wondered what this celestial river was. We now know it's our inside view of a vast rotating disk of 100-400 billion stars.\n\nBut the Milky Way is just one island in an ocean of galaxies. The observable universe contains at least 200 billion galaxies, each with billions of stars. Some are majestic spirals like ours; others are smooth ellipticals or chaotic irregulars. They cluster together in groups, superclusters, and vast cosmic filaments.\n\nGalaxies are the fundamental units of cosmic structure—the building blocks of the universe on the largest scales. Their collisions, mergers, and evolution tell the story of cosmic history."
