                                    type: "concept",
                                    title: "Galaxy Types: The Hubble Sequence",
                                    content: "Edwin Hubble classified galaxies by their shapes—a scheme still used today:\n\n**Spiral Galaxies** (like the Milky Way)\n• Flat disk with spiral arms wrapping outward\n• Central bulge of older stars\n• Active star formation in arms (blue stars, nebulae)\n• 60-70% of bright galaxies\n• Subtypes: Sa (tight arms, large bulge) to Sc (loose arms, small bulge)\n\n**Barred Spirals** (like the Milky Way, actually)\n• Central bar-shaped structure\n• Arms emerge from bar ends\n• Bar may funnel gas to center, feeding black hole\n\n**Elliptical Galaxies**\n• Smooth, featureless spheres or ellipsoids\n• Old, red stars; little gas or star formation\n• Range from dwarf ellipticals to giant ellipticals (largest galaxies)\n• Common in dense galaxy clusters\n\n**Irregular Galaxies**\n• No distinct shape\n• Often result of gravitational interactions\n• Active star formation\n• Examples: Magellanic Clouds"
