                                    type: "concept",
                                    title: "Exoplanets: Worlds Beyond",
                                    content: "As of 2024, we've confirmed over 5,500 exoplanets—and the count grows daily:\n\n**Detection Methods:**\n• **Transit**: Planet crosses in front of star, dimming it slightly (Kepler, TESS)\n• **Radial velocity**: Star wobbles due to planet's gravity\n• **Direct imaging**: Blocking starlight to see planets (difficult)\n• **Gravitational microlensing**: Planet's gravity bends light\n\n**What We've Found:**\n• Planets are common—most stars have them\n• Hot Jupiters: Gas giants orbiting very close to stars\n• Super-Earths: Rocky planets larger than Earth\n• Water worlds: Planets potentially covered in oceans\n• Rogue planets: Wandering without a star\n\n**Potentially Habitable Worlds:**\n• **Proxima Centauri b**: Nearest exoplanet, in habitable zone (4.2 ly)\n• **TRAPPIST-1 system**: 7 Earth-sized planets, 3 in habitable zone (40 ly)\n• **Kepler-442b**: One of most Earth-like known (1,200 ly)\n\n**James Webb Space Telescope** can analyze exoplanet atmospheres for biosignatures."
