                                    type: "concept",
                                    title: "The Fermi Paradox",
                                    content: "If the universe is so vast and old, and habitable planets are common... **where is everybody?**\n\nPhysicist Enrico Fermi posed this question in 1950. The galaxy is 13 billion years old. Even at slow speeds, a civilization could colonize the entire Milky Way in a few million years. Yet we see no evidence of alien civilizations.\n\n**Possible Explanations:**\n\n**They don't exist:**\n• Intelligent life is extremely rare (rare Earth hypothesis)\n• Civilizations destroy themselves quickly (Great Filter)\n• We're the first technological civilization\n\n**They exist but we don't see them:**\n• They're too far away / not interested in expansion\n• They're avoiding us (Zoo hypothesis)\n• They're here but we don't recognize them\n• They use technology we can't detect\n\n**We haven't looked hard enough:**\n• SETI has only searched a tiny fraction of possibilities\n• Radio might be the wrong medium\n• We've only been looking for ~60 years\n\nThe Fermi Paradox reminds us how much we don't know about life in the universe."
