                                    type: "concept",
                                    title: "SETI: Listening for Signals",
                                    content: "The **Search for Extraterrestrial Intelligence** systematically scans the skies for artificial signals:\n\n**Methods:**\n• **Radio SETI**: Searching for narrow-band radio signals (unnatural)\n• **Optical SETI**: Looking for laser pulses\n• **Artifact SETI**: Searching for megastructures or probes\n\n**Notable Projects:**\n• **Project Ozma** (1960): First SETI search, Drake listened to two stars\n• **Wow! Signal** (1977): Strong signal, never repeated—origin unknown\n• **Breakthrough Listen**: Largest SETI program, scanning 1 million stars\n• **SETI@home**: Distributed computing project (1999-2020)\n\n**Challenges:**\n• Where to look? (Billions of stars, infinite frequencies)\n• When to look? (Signals must align with our listening)\n• What to look for? (We assume radio/optical, but aliens might use something else)\n\n**What If We Detect Something?**\n• International protocols exist for announcement\n• Responding would require global consensus\n• A detection—even of an ancient signal—would transform humanity's self-image"
