                                    type: "concept",
                                    title: "The Radio Boy: Where It All Began",
                                    content: "As a young boy in Far Rockaway, New York, Feynman discovered something magical: **understanding how things work is even more fun than using them.**\n\nHis first business? Fixing radios. At age 11, he set up a lab in his room and started repairing neighbors' broken radios. But here's what made him different:\n\n• **He didn't just fix them**—he wanted to understand WHY they broke\n• **He experimented constantly**—what happens if I switch these wires?\n• **He taught himself**—no YouTube tutorials, just manuals and tinkering\n• **He noticed patterns**—same problems, different radios\n\nOne famous story: A man brought in a radio that made terrible noise when it first turned on. Young Richard sat there, thinking. The man got impatient: 'What are you doing? You're just sitting there!' Feynman was thinking about the problem systematically—and he fixed it.\n\n**The lesson:** Real problem-solving starts with thinking, not immediately doing.",
