                                    type: "concept",
                                    title: "The Rules of Scientific Integrity",
                                    content: "Feynman laid out specific rules for not fooling yourself:\n\n**1. Report everything**\n'Details that could throw doubt on your interpretation must be given, if you know them.'\n\n**2. Report all methods**\nOthers must be able to replicate your work. Don't hide the steps that seem unimportant.\n\n**3. Try to think of alternatives**\n'If you're doing an experiment, you should report everything that you think might make it invalid—not only what you think is right.'\n\n**4. Don't fool yourself with statistics**\nData can be tortured until it confesses to anything. Be suspicious of your own analysis.\n\n**5. Lean over backwards**\n'Lean over backwards to show how you're maybe wrong. This is our responsibility as scientists, certainly to other scientists, and I think to laymen.'\n\n**The golden standard:** Could your worst enemy find a flaw in your reasoning? If not, you haven't tried hard enough.",
