                                    content: "**1. Pick one thing you've only read about**\nSomething you think you understand from books, videos, or articles. Now go DO it.\n\n**2. Build before you buy**\nBefore purchasing a solution, try building a basic version yourself. You'll understand the problem 10x better.\n\n**3. Explain through demonstration**\nDon't just tell someone how something works—SHOW them while explaining. Teach by doing.\n\n**4. Keep a 'experiments' list**\nMaintain a running list of small experiments you want to try. Make curiosity actionable.\n\n**5. Follow the ant method**\nWhen curious about something, observe it directly. Spend an hour just watching, timing, testing. No books, no internet—just direct observation.\n\n**6. Mess things up deliberately**\nBreak things to understand them. What happens if I remove this? Change that? Wrong inputs teach as much as right ones.\n\n**Feynman's approach:** Don't ask 'What should I learn?' Ask 'What should I TRY?'",
