                                    type: "example",
                                    title: "A Day in the Feynman Mindset",
                                    content: "What does integrated Feynman-thinking look like in practice?\n\n**Morning:** You're stuck on a problem at work. Instead of Googling for a quick answer, you ask: 'What are the fundamentals here?' You sketch it out, break it into components.\n\n**Commute:** You notice something odd—why do some clouds have flat bottoms? Instead of forgetting it, you jot it down. Tonight you'll look it up. (It's where moist air meets the dew point!)\n\n**At Work:** A coworker proposes a solution. You don't just nod—you ask 'Help me understand why approach A rather than B?' Not to be difficult, but because you genuinely want to learn.\n\n**Evening:** You try something new—maybe cooking an unfamiliar recipe. You don't just follow instructions; you ask WHY certain steps matter. You experiment.\n\n**Throughout:** You catch yourself pretending to understand something. You stop, admit confusion, and dig deeper.\n\n**The feeling:** Not exhausting, but energizing. Every day has moments of discovery.",
