                                    type: "intro",
                                    title: "The Cell That Changed Everything",
                                    content: "In 1906, Santiago RamÃ³n y Cajal proved that the brain isn't a continuous web but consists of individual cells—neurons—that communicate across tiny gaps. This 'neuron doctrine' revolutionized our understanding of the brain. Each of your 86 billion neurons is a sophisticated information processor that receives thousands of inputs, integrates them, and decides whether to send its own signal. Let's explore the architecture of these remarkable cells."
