                                {
                                    type: "example",
                                    title: "Real Neurons in Action",
                                    content: "**Motor Neuron:**\n• Cell body in spinal cord\n• Axon extends up to 1 meter to muscles\n• Heavily myelinated for fast signals (120 m/s)\n• One motor neuron controls hundreds of muscle fibers\n\n**Purkinje Cell (Cerebellum):**\n• Has the most elaborate dendritic tree of any neuron\n• Receives up to 200,000 synaptic inputs!\n• Critical for motor coordination\n• Each cerebellum has ~15 million Purkinje cells\n\n**Pyramidal Neuron (Cortex):**\n• Triangular cell body\n• One long apical dendrite reaching toward brain surface\n• Basal dendrites spreading horizontally\n• Found in layers of cerebral cortex\n• Primary excitatory neurons of the cortex\n\n**Dopamine Neuron (Substantia Nigra):**\n• Cell body in midbrain\n• Axons project widely to striatum\n• Loss of these neurons â†’ Parkinson's disease"
                                },
                                {
                                    type: "quiz",
                                    question: "What is the function of the myelin sheath?",
                                    options: [
                                        { text: "To receive signals from other neurons", correct: false },
                                        { text: "To insulate the axon and speed up signal transmission", correct: true },
                                        { text: "To store neurotransmitters for release", correct: false },
                                        { text: "To produce proteins for the neuron", correct: false }
                                    ],
                                    explanation: "The myelin sheath is a fatty insulation layer that wraps around the axon. It dramatically increases the speed of electrical signal transmission (from ~1 m/s to 120 m/s) by forcing the signal to 'jump' between gaps called Nodes of Ranvier—a process called saltatory conduction."
                                },
                                {
                                    type: "concept",
                                    title: "Glial Cells: The Unsung Heroes",
                                    content: "**Glia** (Greek for \"glue\") are non-neuronal cells essential for brain function. There are about as many glia as neurons.\n\n**Types in the CNS:**\n\n**Astrocytes** (star-shaped)\n• Regulate blood-brain barrier\n• Supply nutrients to neurons\n• Regulate extracellular ion balance\n• Remove excess neurotransmitters\n• May participate in information processing!\n\n**Oligodendrocytes**\n• Produce myelin in CNS\n• One oligodendrocyte myelinates multiple axons\n\n**Microglia**\n• Brain's immune cells\n• Clear debris and dead cells\n• Prune synapses during development\n• Can become overactive in disease\n\n**In the PNS:**\n• **Schwann cells:** Produce myelin (one cell per segment)\n• **Satellite cells:** Support sensory neurons"
                                },
                                {
                                    type: "quiz",
                                    question: "Which part of the neuron decides whether to fire an action potential?",
                                    options: [
                                        { text: "Dendrites", correct: false },
                                        { text: "Nucleus", correct: false },
                                        { text: "Axon hillock", correct: true },
                                        { text: "Axon terminals", correct: false }
                                    ],
                                    explanation: "The axon hillock, located where the axon emerges from the cell body, is the 'trigger zone' for action potentials. It integrates all incoming signals from dendrites and, if the total depolarization exceeds threshold (-55 mV), initiates an all-or-nothing action potential."
                                },
                                {
                                    type: "takeaway",
                                    title: "Key Takeaways: Neuron Structure",
                                    content: "1. **Neurons have specialized regions:** Dendrites receive, soma integrates, axon transmits, terminals release\n\n2. **Dendritic spines physically encode learning**—they grow, shrink, and change shape based on neural activity\n\n3. **Myelin dramatically accelerates signaling** from ~1 m/s to 120 m/s through saltatory conduction at Nodes of Ranvier\n\n4. **Most brain neurons are multipolar** with many dendrites and one axon; different shapes serve different functions\n\n5. **Glial cells are essential partners** providing support, insulation, immune defense, and possibly participating in computation\n\n6. **Neurons are vulnerable**—they mostly cannot divide, so damage is often permanent"
                                }
