                                {
                                    type: "intro",
                                    title: "The Hard Problem",
                                    content: "Right now, you're having a conscious experience. You're not just processing information—there's something it's LIKE to be you, reading these words. This subjective experience is consciousness, and it remains one of science's greatest mysteries. How do 86 billion neurons, following physical and chemical laws, create the inner movie of your mind? We don't fully know—but neuroscience has revealed fascinating clues about the neural basis of awareness."
                                },
                                {
                                    type: "concept",
                                    title: "What Is Consciousness?",
                                    content: "**Consciousness** = subjective, first-person experience of being aware\n\n**Two aspects:**\n\n**1. Arousal/Wakefulness**\n• Basic alertness level\n• Brainstem reticular formation controls this\n• Ranges from coma â†’ sleep â†’ drowsy â†’ alert\n\n**2. Awareness/Content**\n• WHAT you're conscious of\n• Requires cortical activity\n• Can be external (sights, sounds) or internal (thoughts, feelings)\n\n**Altered states:**\n• Sleep (especially REM—vivid dreams)\n• Anesthesia (loss of awareness with maintained arousal circuits)\n• Meditation (altered content)\n• Psychedelics (dramatically altered content)\n\n**The Hard Problem (David Chalmers):**\nWhy is there subjective experience at all? Why aren't we \"philosophical zombies\" processing information without any inner experience?"
                                },
                                {
                                    type: "visual",
                                    title: "Neural Correlates of Consciousness",
                                    content: "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 700 300\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <rect width=\"700\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/>\n  <text x=\"350\" y=\"25\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\">NEURAL CORRELATES OF CONSCIOUSNESS</text>\n  \n  <!-- Brain outline -->\n  <path d=\"M150,250 Q80,180 90,120 Q100,60 200,50 Q350,40 450,55 Q530,70 560,130 Q580,190 550,240 Q500,270 400,275 Q280,280 180,265 Q150,260 150,250\" fill=\"#6366f1\" fill-opacity=\"0.1\" stroke=\"#6366f1\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  \n  <!-- Prefrontal -->\n  <ellipse cx=\"150\" cy=\"130\" rx=\"50\" ry=\"60\" fill=\"#3b82f6\" fill-opacity=\"0.3\" stroke=\"#3b82f6\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"150\" y=\"125\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#3b82f6\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">Prefrontal</text>\n  <text x=\"150\" y=\"140\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"white\" font-size=\"8\">Executive control</text>\n  <text x=\"150\" y=\"152\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"white\" font-size=\"8\">Self-awareness</text>\n  \n  <!-- Parietal -->\n  <ellipse cx=\"350\" cy=\"100\" rx=\"80\" ry=\"45\" fill=\"#8b5cf6\" fill-opacity=\"0.3\" stroke=\"#8b5cf6\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"350\" y=\"95\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#8b5cf6\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">Parietal Cortex</text>\n  <text x=\"350\" y=\"110\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"white\" font-size=\"8\">Spatial awareness</text>\n  <text x=\"350\" y=\"122\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"white\" font-size=\"8\">Integration</text>\n  \n  <!-- Thalamus -->\n  <ellipse cx=\"320\" cy=\"180\" rx=\"40\" ry=\"25\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" fill-opacity=\"0.4\" stroke=\"#f59e0b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"320\" y=\"178\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">Thalamus</text>\n  <text x=\"320\" y=\"192\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"white\" font-size=\"8\">Gateway</text>\n  \n  <!-- Reticular formation -->\n  <rect x=\"370\" y=\"220\" width=\"80\" height=\"40\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#10b981\" fill-opacity=\"0.3\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"410\" y=\"238\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-size=\"9\" font-weight=\"bold\">Reticular</text>\n  <text x=\"410\" y=\"252\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-size=\"9\" font-weight=\"bold\">Formation</text>\n  \n  <!-- Connections showing thalamocortical loops -->\n  <path d=\"M320,155 Q280,100 200,100\" stroke=\"#ec4899\" stroke-width=\"2\" fill=\"none\" stroke-dasharray=\"3,3\"/>\n  <path d=\"M320,155 Q350,80 400,90\" stroke=\"#ec4899\" stroke-width=\"2\" fill=\"none\" stroke-dasharray=\"3,3\"/>\n  <path d=\"M320,155 Q400,140 450,130\" stroke=\"#ec4899\" stroke-width=\"2\" fill=\"none\" stroke-dasharray=\"3,3\"/>\n  \n  <!-- Label for connections -->\n  <text x=\"450\" y=\"70\" fill=\"#ec4899\" font-size=\"9\">Thalamocortical</text>\n  <text x=\"450\" y=\"82\" fill=\"#ec4899\" font-size=\"9\">loops (40 Hz)</text>\n  \n  <!-- Key theories -->\n  <rect x=\"540\" y=\"80\" width=\"150\" height=\"180\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\" stroke=\"#64748b\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n  <text x=\"615\" y=\"100\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">THEORIES:</text>\n  \n  <text x=\"550\" y=\"125\" fill=\"#3b82f6\" font-size=\"9\" font-weight=\"bold\">Global Workspace</text>\n  <text x=\"550\" y=\"140\" fill=\"#94a3b8\" font-size=\"8\">Info broadcast to</text>\n  <text x=\"550\" y=\"152\" fill=\"#94a3b8\" font-size=\"8\">all brain areas</text>\n  \n  <text x=\"550\" y=\"175\" fill=\"#8b5cf6\" font-size=\"9\" font-weight=\"bold\">Integrated Info (IIT)</text>\n  <text x=\"550\" y=\"190\" fill=\"#94a3b8\" font-size=\"8\">Consciousness = Î¦</text>\n  <text x=\"550\" y=\"202\" fill=\"#94a3b8\" font-size=\"8\">(integration)</text>\n  \n  <text x=\"550\" y=\"225\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-size=\"9\" font-weight=\"bold\">Higher-Order</text>\n  <text x=\"550\" y=\"240\" fill=\"#94a3b8\" font-size=\"8\">Awareness of</text>\n  <text x=\"550\" y=\"252\" fill=\"#94a3b8\" font-size=\"8\">mental states</text>\n</svg>"
                                },
                                {
                                    type: "concept",
                                    title: "Theories of Consciousness",
                                    content: "**Global Workspace Theory (Baars, Dehaene)**\n• Consciousness = information broadcast to whole brain\n• \"Spotlight\" of attention makes info available globally\n• Unconscious processes are localized; conscious ones are distributed\n• Prefrontal-parietal network is the \"workspace\"\n\n**Integrated Information Theory (Tononi)**\n• Consciousness = integrated information (Î¦, \"phi\")\n• More integration = more consciousness\n• Explains why cerebellum (more neurons) isn't conscious—modular, not integrated\n• Makes consciousness a fundamental property\n\n**Higher-Order Theories**\n• Consciousness requires representing your own mental states\n• Being aware THAT you're seeing, not just seeing\n• Prefrontal cortex creates this meta-awareness\n\n**Predictive Processing**\n• Brain is a prediction machine\n• Consciousness is the best prediction of sensory causes\n• Explains perception, action, even sense of self"
                                },
                                {
                                    type: "concept",
                                    title: "Attention: The Spotlight of Consciousness",
                                    content: "**Attention** = selective processing of information\n\n**Types of Attention:**\n\n**Selective Attention**\n• Focus on one thing, filter out rest\n• Cocktail party effect: Hear your name across noisy room\n• Inattentional blindness: Miss gorilla walking through basketball game!\n\n**Sustained Attention (Vigilance)**\n• Maintaining focus over time\n• Requires effort; fatigues\n• Air traffic controllers, security screening\n\n**Divided Attention**\n• Splitting focus across tasks\n• Very limited capacity\n• Multitasking is mostly myth (rapid switching)\n\n**Executive Attention**\n• Resolving conflict between responses\n• Inhibiting automatic responses\n• Prefrontal cortex critical\n\n**Brain Networks:**\n• Dorsal attention network (voluntary focus)\n• Ventral attention network (stimulus-driven capture)\n• Default mode network (mind-wandering)"
                                },
                                {
                                    type: "example",
                                    title: "Failures of Consciousness and Attention",
                                    content: "**Inattentional Blindness:**\nIn famous experiment, 50% of people watching a basketball video completely miss a person in a gorilla suit walking through the scene. You don't see what you're not attending to!\n\n**Change Blindness:**\nLarge changes in visual scenes go unnoticed if they occur during a disruption (blink, camera cut). The world feels continuous but your perception is constructed.\n\n**Blindsight:**\nPatients with V1 damage claim to be blind but can still respond to visual stimuli at above-chance levels. Processing without awareness—dissociates vision from consciousness.\n\n**Split-Brain Patients:**\nCorpus callosum cut â†’ two independent consciousness streams. Left hemisphere doesn't know what right is experiencing. Suggests consciousness requires integration.\n\n**Anesthesia:**\nGeneral anesthesia disrupts the thalamocortical loops that support consciousness. Patients later report \"nothing\"—not even time passing. Consciousness is fragile!"
                                },
                                {
                                    type: "concept",
                                    title: "The Default Mode Network",
                                    content: "When you're NOT focused on external tasks, a network called the **Default Mode Network (DMN)** activates.\n\n**Key Regions:**\n• Medial prefrontal cortex\n• Posterior cingulate cortex\n• Angular gyrus\n• Hippocampus\n\n**Functions:**\n• **Mind-wandering:** Daydreaming, random thoughts\n• **Self-referential thinking:** Thinking about yourself\n• **Autobiographical memory:** Remembering your past\n• **Theory of mind:** Thinking about others' mental states\n• **Future planning:** Mental time travel\n\n**Deactivates during focused tasks:**\nWhen you concentrate, DMN quiets down; task-positive networks activate.\n\n**Clinical Relevance:**\n• Depression: Overactive DMN, excessive rumination\n• Meditation: Reduces DMN activity\n• Alzheimer's: DMN regions affected early\n• Psychedelics: Disrupt DMN â†’ ego dissolution"
                                },
                                {
                                    type: "quiz",
                                    question: "According to Global Workspace Theory, what distinguishes conscious from unconscious processing?",
                                    options: [
                                        { text: "Processing speed", correct: false },
                                        { text: "Information is broadcast widely across the brain", correct: true },
                                        { text: "Activity in the brainstem", correct: false },
                                        { text: "Emotional content of the information", correct: false }
                                    ],
                                    explanation: "Global Workspace Theory proposes that consciousness arises when information is broadcast widely across the brain, making it available to multiple processing systems. Unconscious processing remains localized. The prefrontal-parietal 'global workspace' acts as the hub for this broadcasting."
                                },
                                {
                                    type: "quiz",
                                    question: "What is the Default Mode Network primarily associated with?",
                                    options: [
                                        { text: "Intense focus on external tasks", correct: false },
                                        { text: "Motor control and coordination", correct: false },
                                        { text: "Mind-wandering and self-referential thought", correct: true },
                                        { text: "Basic arousal and wakefulness", correct: false }
                                    ],
                                    explanation: "The Default Mode Network activates when we're not focused on external tasks—during mind-wandering, daydreaming, thinking about ourselves, remembering the past, and imagining the future. It deactivates during focused attention. Overactivity is associated with rumination and depression."
                                },
                                {
                                    type: "takeaway",
                                    title: "Key Takeaways: Consciousness and Attention",
                                    content: "1. **Consciousness has two aspects:** Arousal (wakefulness, brainstem) and awareness (content, cortex)\n\n2. **Leading theories:** Global Workspace (broadcast), Integrated Information (Î¦), Higher-Order (meta-awareness)\n\n3. **Attention is the gate to consciousness:** You don't consciously perceive what you don't attend to (inattentional blindness)\n\n4. **The Default Mode Network activates during rest:** Mind-wandering, self-reflection, autobiographical memory\n\n5. **Consciousness requires integration:** Split-brain patients show separate consciousness streams; anesthesia disrupts integrating loops\n\n6. **The Hard Problem remains unsolved:** How does subjective experience arise from physical brain processes?"
                                }
