                                    type: "example",
                                    title: "Clinical Insight: Why Atrial Fibrillation Matters",
                                    content: "In **atrial fibrillation (AFib)**, the atria quiver chaotically instead of contracting effectively:\n\n**What's Lost:**\n• The 'atrial kick' - that final 20% of ventricular filling\n• Coordinated atrial contraction\n\n**Clinical Consequences:**\n• **Reduced cardiac output** - Especially during exercise when that 20% matters more\n• **Blood stasis** - Pooled blood in the atrial appendage can form clots\n• **Stroke risk** - Clots can embolize to the brain (5x increased risk)\n• **Rapid/irregular ventricular response** - Heart rate often 100-180 bpm\n\n**Why Anticoagulation?**\nPatients with AFib often need blood thinners (warfarin, DOACs) because blood stasis in the fibrillating atria creates a high risk of clot formation. The left atrial appendage is particularly prone to clot formation."
