                                    type: "concept",
                                    title: "Bone Remodeling: Wolff's Law",
                                    content: "**Wolff's Law** states that bone adapts to the loads placed upon it:\n\n**The Remodeling Process:**\n1. **Osteoclasts** resorb old/damaged bone (takes ~3 weeks)\n2. **Osteoblasts** deposit new bone (takes ~3 months)\n3. Continuous cycle replaces ~10% of skeleton yearly\n\n**Mechanical Loading Effects:**\n• **Increased load** â†’ Bone becomes denser and stronger\n• **Decreased load** â†’ Bone becomes weaker (disuse atrophy)\n• Astronauts lose 1-2% bone mass per month in space!\n\n**Optimal Loading for Bone Health:**\n• **Impact activities** most effective (running, jumping)\n• **Resistance training** increases bone density\n• **Must exceed normal daily loads** to stimulate adaptation\n• **Progressive overload** principle applies to bones too\n\n**Clinical Note:**\nStress fractures occur when remodeling can't keep pace with accumulated damage—often from too-rapid training increases."
