                                    content: "Joints are classified by the tissue that connects the bones:\n\n**Fibrous Joints (Synarthroses)**\n• Connected by fibrous connective tissue\n• Little to no movement\n• Examples: Skull sutures, tooth sockets\n\n**Cartilaginous Joints (Amphiarthroses)**\n• Connected by cartilage\n• Limited movement\n• Examples: Intervertebral discs, pubic symphysis\n\n**Synovial Joints (Diarthroses)**\n• Most common and most movable\n• Contain **synovial fluid** for lubrication\n• Surrounded by **joint capsule**\n• Examples: Knee, shoulder, hip, elbow\n\n**Synovial Joint Components:**\n• **Articular cartilage** - Smooth, shock-absorbing surface\n• **Synovial membrane** - Produces synovial fluid\n• **Joint capsule** - Encloses the joint\n• **Ligaments** - Provide stability\n• **Bursae** - Fluid-filled sacs reducing friction"
