                                    type: "concept",
                                    title: "Synovial Joint Types and Movements",
                                    content: "**Six types of synovial joints based on shape:**\n\n**Ball and Socket** (Shoulder, Hip)\n• Most mobile joint type\n• Movements: Flexion, extension, abduction, adduction, rotation, circumduction\n• Trade-off: High mobility = less stability\n\n**Hinge** (Elbow, Knee, Ankle, Fingers)\n• Uniaxial - one plane of movement\n• Movements: Flexion and extension only\n• Very stable design\n\n**Pivot** (Atlas/Axis, Proximal Radioulnar)\n• Uniaxial - rotation around one axis\n• Movements: Rotation (supination/pronation)\n• Example: Turning head 'no'\n\n**Condyloid/Ellipsoid** (Wrist, MCP joints)\n• Biaxial - two planes of movement\n• Movements: Flexion, extension, abduction, adduction\n• No rotation\n\n**Saddle** (Thumb CMC joint)\n• Biaxial with greater mobility\n• Movements: Like condyloid plus opposition\n• Allows thumb's unique grip ability\n\n**Gliding/Plane** (Intercarpal, Intertarsal, Facet)\n• Flat surfaces slide on each other\n• Limited movement but crucial for flexibility"
