                                    title: "Muscle Attachments and Actions",
                                    content: "Understanding how muscles create movement:\n\n**Attachment Points:**\n• **Origin** - The more stable, proximal attachment\n• **Insertion** - The more mobile, distal attachment\n• Muscle contraction pulls insertion toward origin\n\n**Muscle Roles in Movement:**\n• **Agonist (Prime mover)** - Primary muscle creating the movement\n• **Antagonist** - Opposes the agonist; controls/slows movement\n• **Synergist** - Assists the agonist\n• **Stabilizer (Fixator)** - Holds structures steady during movement\n\n**Example - Bicep Curl:**\n• Agonist: Biceps brachii\n• Antagonist: Triceps brachii\n• Synergists: Brachialis, brachioradialis\n• Stabilizers: Rotator cuff, core muscles\n\n**Types of Muscle Contractions:**\n• **Concentric** - Muscle shortens under tension (lifting)\n• **Eccentric** - Muscle lengthens under tension (lowering)\n• **Isometric** - No length change (holding position)"
