                                    type: "concept",
                                    title: "Levers in the Human Body",
                                    content: "The musculoskeletal system operates as a series of levers:\n\n**Lever Components:**\n• **Fulcrum** - The joint (axis of rotation)\n• **Effort** - Force applied by muscle\n• **Load/Resistance** - Weight being moved\n• **Lever arm** - Distance from fulcrum to force\n\n**Mechanical Advantage:**\n• **MA = Effort arm / Resistance arm**\n• MA > 1: Favors force (can lift heavier loads)\n• MA < 1: Favors speed and range (faster movement)\n\n**Most body levers have MA < 1:**\n• Muscles attach close to joints\n• Resistance (limb + load) is farther from joint\n• Result: Speed advantage over force\n• Trade-off: Muscles must produce MORE force than the load\n\n**Example - Bicep Curl:**\n• Bicep attaches ~5 cm from elbow\n• Weight held ~35 cm from elbow\n• MA = 5/35 = 0.14\n• Bicep must produce 7× the load force!"
