                                    type: "concept",
                                    title: "Base Rate Neglect",
                                    content: "**Base rate neglect** is ignoring general probabilities in favor of specific information. If a test is 99% accurate and 1% of people have a disease, a positive result actually means only ~50% chance you're sick (because false positives from the 99% healthy outnumber true positives from the 1% sick). We focus on the 99% accuracy and ignore the 1% base rate."
